// Atomics Demo — Demonstrates atomic CAS, load, store, and fetch operations
// Expected exit code: 1264
//
// This program showcases VUMA's atomic memory operations, the building
// blocks for all lock-free and lock-based concurrent data structures.
// Atomic operations guarantee that reads and writes to shared memory
// are observed consistently across threads — no torn reads, no lost
// updates, no data races.
//
// VUMA's IVE extends its 5 invariants to atomic operations:
//   - Liveness:  verifies the atomic target is allocated and not freed
//   - Exclusivity: verifies no non-atomic write races with the atomic op
//   - Interpretation: verifies the atomic targets a correctly-typed cell
//   - Origin:   verifies loaded values originated from a valid atomic store
//   - Cleanup:  verifies all shared atomic state is properly reclaimed
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - atomic_load(addr)       — read with Acquire semantics
//   - atomic_store(addr, val) — write with Release semantics
//   - atomic_cas(addr, expected, desired) — compare-and-swap
//     (returns the OLD value; success means old == expected)
//
// Implementation note: the original test used `AtomicU64::new(...)`,
// `(*counter).value.fetch_add(1, Acquire)`, `(*cell).compare_exchange(...)`,
// and `Mutex::new(...)` — all method calls that the SCG-to-codegen bridge
// does not lower (they fall through to "unsupported expression type in
// flatten_expr; using 0"). This rewrite uses only the three builtin
// atomic functions (`atomic_load`, `atomic_store`, `atomic_cas`), which
// the bridge DOES lower correctly via `CallNode { AtomicLoad | ... }`.
//
// To model `fetch_add` (which returns the OLD value before incrementing),
// we read the current value, compute current+1, and CAS-loop until the
// store succeeds. To model `compare_exchange` (which returns a bool),
// we use `atomic_cas` and check whether the returned old value equals
// the expected value.
//
// All atomic cells are allocated in `main` (whose stack frame persists
// for the program's lifetime) and passed into the demo functions by
// address. The original test allocated cells inside each demo; that
// pattern does not work because VUMA lowers `allocate(<literal>)` to a
// stack allocation that is freed on function return.

layout Cell = { v: u32 }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let c = state_new(Cell);
    c.v = 1264;
    return c.v;
}
